3.text:
 Most templates are wrappers around a repository under nixcafe-develop. Each wrapper sets description, path, and welcomeText: # templates/node/default.nix
{ inputs, ... }:
{
  description = "Node.js development environment with Node.js, Bun, Deno, and ni";
  path = "${inputs.templates-node}";
  welcomeText = ''
    # Node.js Template
    Run `nix develop` to enter the dev shell.
  '';
} For these templates, path points at a templates-* input in the root flake.nix. The input is declared with flake = false, so the template repository is fetched as plain source rather than as a flake. The purr and purr-flake-parts templates work differently. Their path values point at inputs.purr/template/default and inputs.purr/template/flake-parts, so they use the purr flake input and have no separate template input. The root flake.nix has 14 inputs: nixpkgs, purr, git-hooks, and 11 templates-* inputs. It calls inputs.purr.lib.mkFlake with src = ./., and purr discovers the templates/, shells/, and checks/ directories: flake.nix
flake.lock
templates/<name>/default.nix
shells/default/default.nix
checks/git-hooks/default.nix See Quick Start for setup instructions and Contributing for the process of adding a template.

13.text:
 Each template is a default.nix file under templates/<name>/: # templates/node/default.nix
{ inputs, ... }:
{
  description = "Node.js development environment with Node.js, Bun, Deno, and ni";
  path = "${inputs.templates-node}";
  welcomeText = ''
    # Node.js Template
    Run `nix develop` to enter the dev shell.
  '';
} path points at the source that nix flake init copies. For most templates, path references a templates-* input in the root flake.nix. These inputs use github:nixcafe-develop/<repository> with flake = false. The templates-secrets input points at github:nixcafe-develop/nix-secrets. The purr and purr-flake-parts templates do not use a templates-* input. They reference inputs.purr/template/default and inputs.purr/template/flake-parts. The root flake.nix uses inputs.purr.lib.mkFlake with src = ./.. purr scans templates/ and exposes each default.nix as templates.<name>. It also discovers the dev shells in shells/ and the git hooks check in checks/.

17.text:
 Create a repository under nixcafe-develop with a flake.nix and the project files that the template should copy. The existing template repositories do not include a flake.lock.Add the repository as a flake input in the root flake.nix: templates-<name> = {
  url = "github:nixcafe-develop/<repo-name>";
  flake = false;
}; Create templates/<name>/default.nix: { inputs, ... }:
{
  description = "Description of your template";
  path = "${inputs.templates-<name>}";
  welcomeText = ''
    # Template Name
    Run `nix develop` to enter the dev shell.
  '';
} The directory name under templates/ becomes the template name. For example, templates/rust/default.nix is used with nix flake init -t beans#rust. Run the checks: nix flake check Open a pull request against main. For a template that reads from another flake input instead of a templates-* input, follow the approach used by templates/purr/default.nix and templates/purr-flake-parts/default.nix. Both reference directories under inputs.purr.
